# Zeraix 2.0 Roadmap

> **Goal: Upgrade Zeraix from `v2.0.0-beta` to the official `v2.0.0` release.**
>
> Zeraix 2.0 is not primarily about adding more AI features. The core objective is to complete the migration to a **Rust-based Agent Runtime** that centrally manages the Agent Loop, Tools, Scheduler, Permissions, Sandbox, Processes, MCP, Sub-agents, and Context.

---

# 1. Overall 2.0 Objectives

## Current Beta Architecture

```text
Electron
├── React / Next.js UI
├── JS Agent Loop
├── Tools
├── MCP
├── Process
└── Rust Runtime
      └── Infrastructure / Parallel Runtime
```

## Target 2.0 Architecture

```text
                    Electron
                       │
                  UI / UX Layer
                       │
                  JSON-RPC / IPC
                       │
                       ▼
              ┌──────────────────┐
              │ Rust Agent       │
              │ Runtime          │
              └────────┬─────────┘
                       │
          ┌────────────┼────────────┐
          ▼            ▼            ▼
      Agent Core   Scheduler    Context
          │            │            │
          └────────────┼────────────┘
                       ▼
                  Tool Runtime
                       │
             ┌─────────┼─────────┐
             ▼         ▼         ▼
        Permission   Sandbox   Process
             │         │         │
             └─────────┼─────────┘
                       ▼
                  Tool / MCP
                       │
                       ▼
                       OS
```

## Core Acceptance Criteria

Zeraix 2.0 must:

* [ ] Make Rust Runtime the core Agent execution environment
* [ ] Move the Agent Loop into Rust Runtime
* [ ] Move Tool Execution into Rust Runtime
* [ ] Integrate Permission, Sandbox, and Process into one execution pipeline
* [ ] Make Scheduler responsible for Agent Tasks and Sub-agents
* [ ] Make MCP a native Runtime capability
* [ ] Make Context and Compaction independent from the raw chat transcript
* [ ] Decouple Electron from core Agent execution
* [ ] Support reliable long-running and multi-step Agent tasks
* [ ] Pass Security, Stress, and Performance tests

---

# 2. P0 — Rust Agent Runtime

## 2.1 Migrate the Agent Loop to Rust

* [ ] Move the existing JS Agent Loop into Rust Runtime
* [ ] Make Rust Runtime the primary Agent execution entry point
* [ ] Implement the complete Model → Agent → Tool → Result loop
* [ ] Remove duplicated execution logic between JS and Rust
* [ ] Preserve existing Agent behavior during migration
* [ ] Support Agent Task creation
* [ ] Support Agent Task pause
* [ ] Support Agent Task resume
* [ ] Support Agent Task cancellation
* [ ] Support Agent Task completion
* [ ] Support Agent Task failure
* [ ] Support Runtime restart

### Target Flow

```text
Model
  ↓
Rust Agent Core
  ↓
Scheduler
  ↓
Tool Decision
  ↓
Permission
  ↓
Tool Execution
  ↓
Result
  ↓
Model
```

---

# 3. P0 — Tool Execution

## 3.1 File Tools

Migrate the following tools to Rust Runtime:

* [ ] `read_file`
* [ ] `write_file`
* [ ] `edit_file`
* [ ] `search_files`
* [ ] `search_in_files`
* [ ] `list_directory`
* [ ] `file_info`

## 3.2 Command Execution

* [ ] Move Command Execution to Rust
* [ ] Shell management
* [ ] Working directory management
* [ ] Environment variable management
* [ ] stdout capture
* [ ] stderr capture
* [ ] Exit code handling
* [ ] Timeout
* [ ] Cancellation
* [ ] Process Group management

## 3.3 Tool Runtime

* [ ] Tool Registry
* [ ] Tool Schema
* [ ] Tool Validation
* [ ] Tool Timeout
* [ ] Tool Cancellation
* [ ] Tool Retry
* [ ] Tool Error Recovery
* [ ] Tool Concurrency
* [ ] Tool Execution ID
* [ ] Standardized Tool Result
* [ ] Tool Event Streaming

### Required Execution Pipeline

Every Tool must follow:

```text
Agent
 ↓
Tool Request
 ↓
Validation
 ↓
Permission
 ↓
Sandbox
 ↓
Execution
 ↓
Result
```

Core Tool Execution must no longer bypass Rust Runtime through direct Electron / JavaScript execution.

